const eras = [
  ["1931 to 1933", "The 12-Fret Beginning", "The first D-18s belong to Martin's original 12-fret dreadnought tradition: slotted headstock, long body, forward bridge location, mahogany back and sides, and spruce top. Production was small."],
  ["1934 to mid-1938", "The Forward-Braced 14-Fret Era", "The 14-fret body becomes standard. Red spruce, scalloped forward-positioned X-bracing, hide glue, a 1 3/4-inch nut, steel T-bar reinforcement, ebony board and bridge, and light construction define the most coveted recipe."],
  ["Late 1938 to mid-1939", "Rear-Shifted, Wide Neck", "Martin moves the X-brace intersection rearward for added top stability. The braces remain scalloped and the neck remains approximately 1 3/4 inches at the nut for a short transitional window."],
  ["Mid-1939 to 1941", "Narrow Neck, Scalloped Braces", "The nut narrows to about 1 11/16 inches. A wider upper transverse brace and the so-called popsicle brace appear, while red spruce and scalloped rear-shifted bracing continue."],
  ["1942 to 1944", "Wartime D-18", "Metal restrictions bring ebony neck reinforcement and lightweight tuner variants, often without post bushings. Red spruce and scalloped bracing remain, making wartime D-18s extremely desirable despite their different neck feel."],
  ["Late 1944", "Scalloping Ends", "The change from scalloped to non-scalloped top bracing occurs during production, not neatly on January 1. Serial number and internal inspection matter more than the calendar year alone."],
  ["1945 to 1946", "Tapered-Brace Transition", "The earliest postwar braces are tapered rather than the heavier straight braces seen later. Red spruce continues into the transition; Sitka becomes normal during 1946. Some necks still carry ebony reinforcement."],
  ["1947 to 1954", "The Postwar Workhorse", "The fingerboard and bridge change from ebony to rosewood in 1947. Sitka spruce, mahogany, rear-positioned non-scalloped bracing, and open-gear tuners define a clear, dry, powerful bluegrass favorite."],
  ["1955 to 1964", "A Fuller Postwar Build", "Pickguard and tuner details evolve, and dreadnought brace geometry changes again around 1958. These guitars remain lightly appointed and musically strong, but generally sell below scalloped or tapered-brace examples."],
  ["1965 to 1969", "Short Saddle and Bridge-Plate Changes", "A short drop-in saddle replaces the long through saddle during the mid-1960s. Binding and pickguard materials change; the small rosewood bridge plate gives way to maple in 1968 before later plates grow larger."],
  ["1970s to 2011", "Heavier Builds and Vintage Revivals", "Larger bridge plates and other production choices can make 1970s examples more variable. Later D-18V, Golden Era, Authentic, and limited models deliberately revive selected prewar specifications."],
  ["2012 to Today", "The Reimagined Standard", "Martin's 2012 redesign restored forward-shifted scalloped bracing, a 1 3/4-inch nut, open-gear tuners, and vintage-influenced trim. Modern specifications are not evidence of a prewar guitar."],
];

const checks = [
  ["Model and Serial Stamp", "Read both stamps on the neck block and date the serial against Martin's year-end chart. A correct number establishes a production year, not originality."],
  ["Top and Back Woods", "A standard D-18 should have a spruce top with mahogany back and sides. Identify red versus Sitka spruce from several construction clues, not color alone."],
  ["X-Brace Position", "Measure the X intersection in relation to the soundhole and inspect the entire brace pattern. Forward and rear positioning changed before scalloping ended."],
  ["Brace Carving", "Distinguish scalloped, tapered, and straight braces. Later aftermarket scalloping is a serious alteration and should not be represented as factory work."],
  ["Neck Reinforcement", "Use a magnet and inspection evidence to distinguish steel T-bar, wartime ebony reinforcement, later square tube, and modern adjustable truss rod construction."],
  ["Bridge and Bridge Plate", "Confirm footprint, wood, saddle slot, pin spacing, thickness, glue, plate size, and repairs. Shaved bridges and oversized plates materially affect function and value."],
  ["Pickguard and Finish", "Check guard material, shrinkage, finish beneath the guard, lacquer checking, overspray, touch-up, and sanding. A refinish can look cleaner than an original guitar."],
  ["Tuners and Footprints", "Match plate shape, screw spacing, post holes, bushings, buttons, plating, and finish shadows to the period. Correct-looking replacements remain replacements."],
  ["Neck Angle and Frets", "A professional neck reset or refret can be appropriate maintenance. Look for heel gaps, altered fingerboard extension, low saddle, excessive action, and poor prior work."],
  ["Cracks and Internal Repairs", "Inspect the bridge area, pickguard crack, center seam, sides, loose braces, cleats, top distortion, and neck block. Repair quality matters more than the mere existence of an old crack."],
];
